/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A solid electrolyte is a solid phase (crystalline ceramic or ion-exchange polymer) that conducts one kind of ion and almost no electrons.
   - σ is the ionic conductivity in S/m; σ₀ is a prefactor in S/m; Ea is the activation energy in J; k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J/K is Boltzmann's constant; T is the temperature in kelvin.
   - Ea is large, so useful conductivity arrives only at a few hundred degrees Celsius.
   - In potentiometric mode two electrodes on the electrolyte see two oxygen partial pressures, p₁ and p₂.
   - Amperometric mode puts a diffusion barrier over the cathode and holds the cell at a voltage that pumps away every oxygen molecule that arrives.
   - A surface acoustic wave (SAW) travels along the surface of a piezoelectric crystal, with its energy confined to about one wavelength of depth.
   - An interdigital transducer (IDT) is two interleaved comb-shaped metal electrodes; the centre-to-centre spacing of neighbouring fingers is half an acoustic wavelength, so the finger pattern repeats every wavelength.
   - f is the operating frequency in Hz; Δf is its shift; v is the surface wave velocity in m/s; Δv is its change, signed; λ is the acoustic wavelength in m, set by the IDT finger spacing.
   - Mass loading or a soft, viscoelastic film on the delay path slows the wave and adds attenuation.
   - In medicine the SAW path becomes an acoustic biosensor.
*/
