/* book exam traps: Applying the Sauerbrey equation, a bulk quartz-crystal-microbalance result, to a SAW delay line, and forgetting that the shift follows mass per unit area, not total mass. | Reversing the Nernst ratio: the EMF rises when the sample oxygen falls, so preference sits on top of the fraction. | Believing a solid electrolyte conducts electrons like a wire. It conducts one ion, and a potentiometric cell still needs a reference gas or electrode. | Mixing the two modes: potentiometric is logarithmic and needs a reference; amperometric with a diffusion barrier is linear and needs none. | Ignoring the temperature dependence of σ. A zirconia cell below about 350 °C gives no usable reading whatever the oxygen level. */
