/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A biosensor has two parts in series: a biological element that recognises one molecule, and a transducer that converts that recognition into a voltage, a current or a light level.
   - The element sets the selectivity.
   - The transducer sets the signal: electrochemical (amperometric, potentiometric or conductometric), optical, acoustic (see solid electrolyte and SAW sensors), calorimetric and FET types.
   - Start with an n-channel MOSFET.
   - The bare oxide carries silanol groups.
   - ΔVT is the threshold shift in volts; k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J/K; T is the absolute temperature in kelvin; q = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. 59 mV per pH is the ideal slope at 25 °C and 61.5 mV at 37 °C.
   - Two errors dominate: drift, as the oxide hydrates over hours, which needs frequent recalibration; and hysteresis, a reading that depends on whether the previous sample was acid or alkali.
   - IMFET (immunologically modified FET).
   - EnFET (enzyme FET).
   - Glucose oxidase (GOx) is the enzyme.
*/
